1️⃣ What we are building (Real-time use case)
✅ Real Production Scenario
You have an application running on EC2 / EKS / ECS that connects to an RDS database.

You want to store:

DB username

DB password
securely without hardcoding them in code or Terraform.

👉 We will store DB credentials in AWS Secrets Manager using Terraform.

2️⃣ Why Secrets Manager (DevOps mindset)
Requirement	Why Secrets Manager
Store sensitive secrets	Encrypted by default
Password rotation	Supported
Audit access	CloudTrail
Secure access	IAM
Production ready	Yes
3️⃣ Terraform Project Structure (Best Practice)
secrets-manager-terraform/
│── provider.tf
│── variables.tf
│── main.tf
│── outputs.tf
│── terraform.tfvars
4️⃣ Configure AWS Provider (provider.tf)
provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"   # Mumbai
}
5️⃣ Define Variables (variables.tf)
variable "environment" {
  type    = string
  default = "prod"
}

variable "db_username" {
  type = string
}

variable "db_password" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
}
6️⃣ Pass Secret Values (terraform.tfvars)
db_username = "app_user"
db_password = "StrongPass@123"
⚠ Add this to .gitignore

terraform.tfvars
7️⃣ Create Secret in AWS Secrets Manager (main.tf)
🔹 Step 1: Create Secret Metadata
resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret" "db_secret" {
  name = "prod/db/credentials"

  description = "Production DB credentials for application"

  recovery_window_in_days = 7
}
🔹 Step 2: Store Secret Value (JSON format)
resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "db_secret_value" {
  secret_id = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db_secret.id

  secret_string = jsonencode({
    username = var.db_username
    password = var.db_password
  })
}
💡 Secrets Manager stores secrets as JSON (recommended)

8️⃣ Outputs (outputs.tf)
output "secret_name" {
  value = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db_secret.name
}
9️⃣ Deploy Step-by-Step
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply
✅ Secret is now created securely.

🔟 Verify in AWS Console
Go to:

AWS Console → Secrets Manager → prod/db/credentials
You’ll see:

{
  "username": "app_user",
  "password": "******"
}
1️⃣1️⃣ How Application Uses This Secret (REAL DevOps Flow)
🔹 IAM Role (EC2 / EKS / ECS)
Attach permission:

{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": "secretsmanager:GetSecretValue",
  "Resource": "arn:aws:secretsmanager:*:*:secret:prod/db/credentials*"
}
🔹 Read Secret using AWS CLI
aws secretsmanager get-secret-value \
  --secret-id prod/db/credentials
🔹 Application Example (runtime)
SECRET=$(aws secretsmanager get-secret-value \
  --secret-id prod/db/credentials \
  --query SecretString \
  --output text)

DB_USER=$(echo $SECRET | jq -r .username)
DB_PASS=$(echo $SECRET | jq -r .password)
👉 App connects to DB using retrieved credentials.

